export default "## Why the Distinction Matters\n\nGetting spotting and period confused has consequences for cycle tracking. If you log spotting as day 1, your recorded cycle length becomes shorter than your actual cycle. Do this consistently and period predictions drift off by the number of days between spotting onset and full flow. This is usually one to three days but sometimes more.\n\nOver several cycles, this makes tracking less useful for understanding your body and for any app's predictions. Getting the definition right from the start saves the need to recalibrate later.\n\nThis guide is for educational purposes only. It is not a substitute for medical advice.\n\n## The Clearest Differences at a Glance\n\n### Volume\n\nSpotting does not require a pad or tampon. It appears as light staining on underwear, a few drops on toilet paper when wiping, or a faint mark on a panty liner. A period, even a light period, produces enough blood to need menstrual protection within a few hours.\n\nIf you need to change a pad or tampon because it is saturated, it is a period. If a panty liner would be excessive, it is spotting.\n\n### Pattern of Flow\n\nA period has a characteristic pattern. It starts light, builds to its heaviest flow in the first one to two days, then tapers off. Even in people with lighter periods, there is a noticeable peak.\n\nSpotting stays consistently minimal. There is no build. The amount on day 1 of spotting is similar to the amount on day 2 or day 3. It does not intensify.\n\n### Color\n\nA fully established period is typically bright red during peak flow, becoming darker and eventually brownish as it winds down.\n\nSpotting is most often pink, light red, rust, or brown. Brownish spotting is particularly common because the small volume of blood moves slowly and oxidizes, changing color from red to brown before it exits the body.\n\nFresh bright red spotting can occur, particularly from cervical contact or ovulation, but it remains spotting as long as the volume is small.\n\n### Duration\n\nSpotting lasts one to three days, and often less. It does not have the characteristic arc of a period. There is no escalation phase.\n\nA period typically lasts three to seven days. Even short periods of two to three days have the buildup-peak-taper pattern that distinguishes them from spotting.\n\n## Types of Spotting and When They Occur\n\n### Pre-Period Spotting (Days Before Flow)\n\nThe most common type of spotting. It appears one to two days before full menstrual flow and represents the beginning of uterine lining breakdown as progesterone drops. It is brown or pinkish, light, and transitions to full flow within 48 hours. Normal and common.\n\nIf spotting begins four or more days before the expected period, that pattern is worth tracking more carefully. It can reflect a short luteal phase, polyps, or hormonal imbalance.\n\n### Mid-Cycle (Ovulation) Spotting\n\nLight spotting around day 10 to 16 of the cycle, often accompanied by a change in cervical mucus and possibly mild one-sided pelvic pain. Caused by the hormonal shift around ovulation. Common in a subset of people and cyclically consistent. Not a concern in isolation.\n\n### Post-Period Spotting (Days After Flow)\n\nBrownish residual spotting for one to two days after the main period ends is common. It is the tail of the period winding down. Distinct spotting that appears several clear days after the period has stopped is a separate event with other possible causes such as cervical ectropion, ovulation, or polyps.\n\n### Breakthrough Bleeding\n\nSpotting or light bleeding on hormonal contraception. Typically light and brief, most common in the first few months of use. Not the same as a period. There is no natural hormonal cycle driving it.\n\n## How to Log It Correctly\n\nWhen you notice bleeding:\n\n1. **Assess volume**, does it require menstrual protection, or is it just traces or staining?\n2. **Note color**, pink, red, brown, or rust\n3. **Record cycle day**, count from the last confirmed day 1 of full flow\n4. **Note whether it builds**, if you check again in a few hours and flow has clearly increased, it is likely becoming a period\n\nIn your tracker, log spotting as spotting, not as day 1, until full flow is established. Then mark that day as day 1.\n\n## The Privacy Consideration\n\nAccurate cycle tracking, including the distinction between spotting and period, creates a detailed reproductive health record over time. When stored on a company's servers, this record can be accessed by the company, sold to data brokers, or produced in response to legal subpoenas. In states with abortion restrictions, law enforcement has actively sought period tracking data.\n\nPeriod tracker apps are not covered by HIPAA. Flo Health's FTC enforcement action in 2021 and $59.5 million class action settlement in 2025 confirmed that major apps shared sensitive health data with third parties without adequate user disclosure.\n\nAn on-device tracker stores your cycle data only on your device. The company building the app never receives it, which means there is no database to query, no data to sell, and no record to produce in legal proceedings. For detailed cycle tracking data, on-device storage is the only architecture that genuinely keeps your data yours.";
